Clauses 

.used to filter, sort, group, and limit data in SQL queries
.building blocks of powerful DB queries
    .act like instructions within a statement, specifying actions like filtering data, sorting results, & grouping info


1. WHERE 
    .filters records based on specific conditions 
    .useful for retrieving or modifying only the required data from a table 
    .used with SELECT, UPDATE, and DELETE statements 
    .supports comparison operators 
    .allows logical operators such as AND, OR, and NOT for combining multiple conditions
    .hels working with specific data instead of entire tables, improving query efficiency and performance



2. HAVING 
    .filters grouped data based on aggregate conditions 
    .used for applying conditions on results of GROUP BY clause 
    .allows conditions on aggregated values like COUNT, SUM, AVG, MAX, MIN
    .works similarly to WHERE but is applied after grouping data


3. ORDER BY
    .sorts the result set in ascending (ASC) or descending (DESC) order
    .can sort by one or more columns
    .used to organize data in a specific order for better readability and analysis



4. GROUP BY
    .groups rows based on specified columns
    .used with aggregate functions to summarize data
    .helps in generating summary reports and insights from data



5. LIMIT
    .restricts the number of rows returned by a query
    .useful for pagination or when only a subset of data is needed
    .can be used with an offset to specify the starting point of the result set



6. PARTITION BY
    .used in window functions to divide result set into partitions
    .allows performing calculations across a set of rows related to the current row
    .useful for ranking, running totals, and moving averages without collapsing rows into groups





